The main thesis of the text is that God creates everything, and humans are vessels for experiencing that creation through subjective consciousness (qualia). It argues that suffering and evil are not objective realities imposed on us, but rather subjective experiences we choose to perceive or believe in. Ultimately, the text suggests that individuals have agency over their experience and can choose to change their perspective or situation.

I do not agree with the text's thesis. While it is true that perception and belief shape our subjective experience of suffering, the text goes too far by reducing objective, systemic harms—such as war, disease, and exploitation—to mere personal choices or "pretending a doll tiger is a real tiger." This perspective risks invalidating the very real, physical suffering of others and ignores the complex, often involuntary nature of trauma and systemic injustice.

Here is a short poem based on the text:

We are vessels, drifting through the light,
Choosing what we see, both day and night.
Though pain may seem a tiger, fierce and real,
It is the lens through which we choose to feel. [end of text]


